{"id":812,"date":"2020-11-06T10:58:18","date_gmt":"2020-11-06T18:58:18","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=812"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:41:34","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:41:34","slug":"battery-sensor","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/battery-sensor\/","title":{"rendered":"4A: Battery"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22166dac32e\" name=\"6a22166dac32e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Battery indicator<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Think of all the electronics that you use and how you know that their battery is low or dead on each of them. If you are on a laptop or phone, you will most likely see a battery icon on the top or bottom of your screen. If you used Blockly to program your CoDrone Mini, you probably saw the battery level on the bottom lefthand corner of the screen.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">How can you tell if your CoDrone Mini is running out of battery in Python though? There are three methods that you can try out: printing out the battery level, limiting when your drone can take off, and programming the LED as an indicator.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22166dac464\" name=\"6a22166dac464\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Printing the battery level<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You used the print function in previous lessons, and you can use it to print your CoDrone Mini\u2019s battery level too! After pairing with your drone and importing the library, call the function <em>get_battery_percentage()<\/em> and store its value in a variable called <em>battery<\/em>. Finally, print the battery level. We added the words &#8220;Battery Level: &#8221; to our print statement to label it for you! If you want to do the same, make sure you add a comma in between all of the print parameters.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nbattery = drone.get_battery_percentage()\r\n\r\nprint(\"Battery Level: \", battery)<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Now run that code again! Did it change? Go up? Go down? Do not be alarmed if you run the code a few times and it seems to change a bit, especially after doing certain flight commands. The battery percentage is an approximation from reading the battery\u00a0<strong>voltage.\u00a0<\/strong>Don&#8217;t worry too much about voltage for now, just be aware that the battery percentage may fluctuate a bit.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22166dac4cd\" name=\"6a22166dac4cd\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Before and after<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]You probably won&#8217;t notice a big difference in the percentage yet, so add some flying!<\/p>\n<p>Print the battery percentage before and after hovering for 10 seconds.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nbattery = drone.get_battery_percentage()\r\nprint(\"Before: \", battery)\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.hover(10)\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\n\r\nbattery = drone.get_battery_percentage()\r\nprint(\"After: \", battery)\r\n\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>What was the difference? Explore with various flight commands to see which one consumes the most energy.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22166dac530\" name=\"6a22166dac530\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Limiting takeoff<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this exercise, you will write a program that will either takeoff or do nothing depending on the battery level.<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">First, include the program that you wrote in the last step which will print out your drone\u2019s battery level. Next, write a conditional so that if the battery level is greater than 50, it will take off, hover for 2 seconds, and land. Else, it will print \u201cNot enough power!\u201d and stay grounded.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\nbattery = drone.get_battery_percentage()\r\n\r\nprint(\"Battery Level: \", battery)\r\n\r\n\r\nif battery &gt; 50:\r\n\r\n    drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.hover(2)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.land()\r\n\r\n\r\nelse:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"Not enough power!\")<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try changing the battery level in the conditional to a different number. At what percentage does your drone really not have enough power to take off, land, and hover?<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22166dac590\" name=\"6a22166dac590\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Adding the LED<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The LED on your CoDrone Mini already acts as a battery indicator &#8212; when your battery is low, it flashes red. In this program, you will be adding more colors to indicate different battery percentages.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">First, include the code from the last two steps that will import libraries, pair with CoDrone Mini, and print the battery percentage. You will also need to import the time library to include the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">time.sleep()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">function in your program.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nbattery = drone.get_battery_percentage()\r\n\r\nprint(\"Battery Level: \", battery)\r\n\r\ntime.sleep(1)\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22166dac606\" name=\"6a22166dac606\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Add conditionals to your LED program<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You are going to use conditionals to check your battery\u2019s percentage. Depending on your battery level, your CoDrone Mini&#8217;s LED will turn a certain color. You can change them to whatever you want, but we recommend you make them easy to tell apart so you know when you need to charge your battery. This is what we&#8217;re going to use for this lesson:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">100-76: solid green LED<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">75-51: solid yellow LED<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">50-26: solid red LED<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">25-0: will not fly<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, write an if statement that will change your CoDrone Mini\u2019s LED to green when the battery level is above 75%. Include a delay so flight movements after the LED change aren\u2019t skipped, and then add a takeoff command.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">if battery &gt; 75:\r\n\r\n    drone.LED_color(0, 255, 0, 100)\u00a0 # green\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 time.sleep(0.05)\u00a0 # delay so takeoff doesn't skip\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.takeoff()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22166dac66d\" name=\"6a22166dac66d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Adding else if statements<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Do the same for 51-75% battery and then 26-50% battery, but with a few changes:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Instead of if statements, use else if statements.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Instead of the battery being greater than 75%, it should be greater than 50% and then 25%.<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">elif battery &gt; 50:\r\n\r\n    drone.LED_color(255,255,0,100) # yellow\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 time.sleep(0.05)\u00a0 # delay so takeoff doesn't skip\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\nelif battery &gt; 25:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.LED_color(255,0,0, 100) # red\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 time.sleep(0.05)\u00a0 # delay so takeoff doesn't skip\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.takeoff()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22166dac6c6\" name=\"6a22166dac6c6\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Adding an else statement and flight commands<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Add an else statement to indicate that if none of the above statements are true and your battery is less than 25%, your drone does not have enough power to fly!<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">else:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0print(\"Not enough power!\")<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">All of the conditionals above 25% have a takeoff command included, so include any flight commands you would like in your program after the else statement. Don\u2019t forget to land and close your drone!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22166dac717\" name=\"6a22166dac717\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Final LED code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your final code should look like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nbattery = drone.get_battery_percentage()\r\n\r\nprint(\"Battery Level: \", battery)\r\n\r\ntime.sleep(1)\r\n\r\n\r\nif battery &gt; 75:\r\n\r\n    drone.LED_color(0, 255, 0, 100)\u00a0 # green\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 time.sleep(0.05)\u00a0 # delay so takeoff doesn't skip\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\nelif battery &gt; 50:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.LED_color(255,255,0,100) # yellow\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 time.sleep(0.05)\u00a0 # delay so takeoff doesn't skip\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\nelif battery &gt; 25:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.LED_color(255,0,0, 100) # red\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0time.sleep(0.05)\u00a0 # delay so takeoff doesn't skip\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\nelse:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"Not enough power!\")\r\n\r\n\r\n# Insert your own flight pattern here\r\n\r\ndrone.set_pitch(100)\r\n\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\ndrone.close()\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22166dac7e5\" name=\"6a22166dac7e5\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<b>Challenge: Low Battery<\/b><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Remember the step where we asked you to figure out when your drone really doesn\u2019t have enough power to fly anymore? For this challenge, instead of just not flying at 25% battery and below, set your LED to blink red (or another color!) but still take off when your battery is above that percentage.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hints:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You will need to include another else if statement for this.\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Set the value in your else if statement to that battery percentage. For example, if your drone cannot fly at 10% battery, your statement should read \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">elif battery &gt; 10<\/code>.\u00a0<\/span><\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Don\u2019t forget to keep that else statement at the end!<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 11 Battery indicator [vc_column_text]Think of all the electronics that you use and how you know that their battery is low or dead on each of them. If you&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/battery-sensor\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1729,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":744,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1770,1793,1826,1482,853,694,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2154,2125,2156,2122,2134,2155],"class_list":["post-812","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-battery","lesson-tag-codrone-mini","lesson-tag-intermediate","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-sensor","module-intermediate","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/812","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":8,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/812\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":2853,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/812\/revisions\/2853"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1729"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=812"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=812"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=812"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}